--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REASONING TRACE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. BUG 1 — Missing 'type' column:
     The SignalCreate schema (what the API receives) contains a 'type' field
     (e.g. "flood", "traffic_blockage"). However, the Signal ORM model (what
     gets written to the DB) had no 'type' column. Every call to
     session.add(db_signal) was silently dropping the type. This means the
     DB had no record of what kind of signal was received — a critical gap
     for the Detection Agent which needs to filter by signal type.
     Fix: Added type: Optional[str] = Field(default=None) after 'source'.
     Optional because future background signals (weather/traffic) may not
     carry a type until the Signal Agent normalizes them.

  2. BUG 2 — Non-nullable lat/lng causing DB insert errors:
     lat: float and lng: float are non-nullable (no default). When a
     Weather API signal (which has no GPS coords) is ingested via
     POST /api/v1/signals, SQLModel tries to insert NULL into non-nullable
     float columns → IntegrityError. This would silently crash every
     weather and traffic signal. The GPS-first architecture still works
     because GPS signals always provide lat/lng; the Optional just stops
     non-GPS signals from erroring.
     Fix: lat: Optional[float] = Field(default=None)
          lng: Optional[float] = Field(default=None)

  3. SCHEMAS — Unsafe 'Any' types on SignalRead:
     id: Any would serialize as a Python UUID object in some contexts and
     a string in others, causing unpredictable API responses. Frontend
     and downstream code expects a consistent UUID string.
     created_at: Any had the same problem — datetime serialization is
     handled correctly by Pydantic V2 when typed as datetime.
     Also aligned SignalRead with the ORM model: added lat/lng as
     Optional[float] and type as Optional[str].

  4. PYDANTIC V2 CONFIG:
     The original code used the V1 inner-class pattern:
       class Config: from_attributes = True
     Pydantic V2 canonical form is:
       model_config = {"from_attributes": True}
     Updated to V2 form to prevent deprecation warnings.

